  "content": "\u003cp\u003eI used to be a projectionist at a 30-plex movie theater. One of the most troublesome things to deal with was when the film would jam (there\u0026#39;s a few ways this can happen, always by human error) and would get stuck in the aperture. Movie theaters projectors use very bright and very hot bulbs, so if the film isn\u0026#39;t moving at a steady 24 frames a second, the film will actually burn. And that image would be shown on screen.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eYes, it looked really cool.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eYes, it was kind of a pain to fix. as you\u0026#39;d have to cut off sections that were damaged (obviously the burned frame). This typically wouldn\u0026#39;t impact the audio or video TOO much since it\u0026#39;s only a couple frames that would get damaged when this happens but obviously you don\u0026#39;t want to make this a habit.\u003c/p\u003e\u003cp\u003eThis video has some examples of what this looks like: \u003ca href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_mPgoyC2M_o\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\"\u003e\u003cspan class=\"invisible\"\u003ehttps://www.\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"\"\u003eyoutube.com/watch?v=_mPgoyC2M_o\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"invisible\"\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e",
